WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:06.000
In 1977, the world was introduced&nbsp;
to the most famous haunted house&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:11.160
in history. The first line of this&nbsp;
not-so-humble-abode’s address - 112&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:17.160
Ocean Avenue - might not ring any bells,&nbsp;
but the second surely will - Amityville.

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:24.480
Prior to 1977, that was the strange name&nbsp;
of a sleepy suburb on the outskirts of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:29.700
New York City. Today, it’s synonymous&nbsp;
with one thing: the Amityville Horror.

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:34.440
That’s the name of a book written by Jay&nbsp;
Anson that chronicles the experiences of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:41.820
the Lutz family, who moved into 112 Ocean&nbsp;
Avenue a week before Christmas in 1975.

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:48.660
Within a few days, the Lutzs started to have&nbsp;
strange, unsettling experiences. They were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:55.140
subtle at first - inexplicable cold spots and foul&nbsp;
odours around the house. And in the basement was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.140 --> 00:01:00.540
a bricked-up room with blood-red walls, which&nbsp;
didn't appear on any of the building plans.

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:05.460
But as the weeks went by, the&nbsp;
paranormal activity escalated.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:10.440
George Lutz woke one night to find&nbsp;
his wife levitating above their bed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:16.200
A creepy demon with the face of a pig was spotted&nbsp;
peering through windows on the ground floor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:22.560
and cloven-hoofed footprints appeared all around&nbsp;
the house after a night of heavy snowfall.

00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:29.400
The family called a Catholic priest to exorcise&nbsp;
whatever entity was haunting them. But as soon as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:35.760
he whipped out the holy water, a disembodied voice&nbsp;
barked ‘get out,’ and he was slapped around the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:41.460
face by an invisible hand. By the time he got home&nbsp;
that evening, fresh blisters covered his skin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:46.980
and he developed wounds on the palms of his&nbsp;
hands that looked suspiciously like stigmata.

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:55.380
Just 28 days after moving into their new&nbsp;
home, the Lutz family left it in terror for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.380 --> 00:02:00.420
their lives and their immortal souls. They&nbsp;
didn't even bother to pack their things.

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:35.820
Like many famous horror stories, this one's&nbsp;
supposed to be true, though for obvious reasons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:41.880
plenty of people have their doubts - more on&nbsp;
that later. But what very few people realise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:49.140
is that the most spine-chilling aspect of the&nbsp;
entire account is, in fact, completely true.&nbsp;

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:54.000
If you've watched the film adaptation of the&nbsp;
Amityville Horror, you'll recall an estate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.000 --> 00:04:00.120
agent informing the Lutz that a mere year&nbsp;
before, 6 people were murdered in the house.

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:08.100
Those murders really did take place, and to this&nbsp;
day, exactly how - and why - they were carried out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:15.780
remains a mystery. Some suspect Mafia involvement,&nbsp;
others believe the killer had help from an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:22.020
unidentified accomplice, and still, others blame&nbsp;
whatever malignant force festers within the house.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.860 --> 00:04:30.180
But one thing is for certain - in this case,&nbsp;
the reality really is far scarier than fiction.

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:34.320
This is the true story of the Amityville Horror.

00:04:35.940 --> 00:04:42.060
In the early hours of the 13th of November,&nbsp;
1974, 23-year-old Ronald 'Butch' DeFeo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:47.040
left his home at 112 Ocean Avenue. He&nbsp;
worked at his father's car dealership,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:51.060
but as the son of the boss, his main&nbsp;
responsibility was to sit around on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:55.560
his arse doing bugger-all all day, before&nbsp;
clocking off early to head to the pub.

00:04:55.560 --> 00:05:01.140
Unlike his son, Butch's father was usually&nbsp;
pretty conscientious, but for some reason,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.140 --> 00:05:07.800
that particular morning he failed to show up for&nbsp;
work. It wasn't like Ronald's senior to take a day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:13.140
off, and it was practically unheard of for him to&nbsp;
do so without telling someone . Butch called home&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:19.950
from the dealership to find out what was going on,&nbsp;
but nobody answered the phone. That was odd too -&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.950 --> 00:05:25.560
Butch lived with his parents and his 4 siblings,&nbsp;
and there was almost always someone at home.

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:30.540
Butch was befuddled but went&nbsp;
on with his day. As usual,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:34.980
he left early and went to see his girlfriend&nbsp;
before heading to the local bar to meet some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:40.020
friends around 6pm. Throughout the day he called&nbsp;
home periodically, but there was still no answer.

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:47.640
By this point Butch was beginning to get worried,&nbsp;
so he left his friends at the bar and walked the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:52.320
five or so minutes home. The family's cars&nbsp;
were parked in the drive, but the front door&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:58.140
was locked and when he rang the doorbell,&nbsp;
nobody answered. Butch didn't have a key,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:01.800
so he nipped round the back of the house&nbsp;
and climbed in through an unlocked window.

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:07.020
Inside he soon discovered why nobody&nbsp;
had been answering the phone all day:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.020 --> 00:06:10.680
every member of his family was dead.

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:18.060
Shortly thereafter, the police turned up to&nbsp;
a grizzly scene. Butch's parents and his four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:23.460
siblings were all found lying face down in&nbsp;
their beds, shot by an unknown assailant.

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:29.940
Amityville was simply not the kind&nbsp;
of place where such horrors happened,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:33.780
and the local police were baffled -&nbsp;
there was no sign of a forced entry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.780 --> 00:06:39.300
and nothing had been stolen. Struggling to see&nbsp;
a motive for the crime, they asked Butch - who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.300 --> 00:06:44.340
was now sobbing uncontrollably - whether he knew&nbsp;
anyone who might have wanted to hurt his family.

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:49.620
As it happened, he did. Butch's great&nbsp;
uncle, Peter DeFeo, was a Caporegime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.620 --> 00:06:55.140
in the Genovese crime family - in other&nbsp;
words, he was Mafia. A few years earlier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.140 --> 00:07:00.060
one of Peter's criminal colleagues -&nbsp;
hitman Louis Fallini - had laid low at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:04.140
the DeFeo residence for several weeks.&nbsp;
During that time, Fallini and Butch's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.140 --> 00:07:09.540
father apparently fell out. Butch thought&nbsp;
the murders must be some kind of payback.

00:07:10.380 --> 00:07:14.400
Fearing Fallini might target&nbsp;
the sole DeFoe family survivor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:17.580
Butch was taken into custody&nbsp;
for his own protection.

00:07:19.140 --> 00:07:24.120
The police, meanwhile, began searching the house&nbsp;
for clues, and it wasn't long before they made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.120 --> 00:07:29.400
a breakthrough. To their surprise, the vital&nbsp;
evidence wasn't found near any of the bodies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:35.340
but in the only bedroom the killer&nbsp;
apparently hadn’t visited - Butch's.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.340 --> 00:07:40.440
Tucked away in a corner were a couple of&nbsp;
empty cardboard boxes, which turned out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.440 --> 00:07:47.880
to be the packaging for two Marlin rifles, one a&nbsp;
.22 calibre and the other .35. And sure enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.880 --> 00:07:53.760
the police later confirmed the murder&nbsp;
weapon was a .35 calibre Marlin rifle.

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:58.920
Unsurprisingly, suspicion befell&nbsp;
Butch, and his story started to shatter

00:07:58.920 --> 00:08:02.880
In his statement, he'd told the police&nbsp;
exactly what I just told you - that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:07.080
he got up early and went to work, then&nbsp;
visited his girlfriend, went to the bar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.080 --> 00:08:13.680
and finally discovered his entire family&nbsp;
dead in their beds. That was plausible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:19.020
enough and Butch had solid alibis for all&nbsp;
of it... but there was one small problem.

00:08:19.020 --> 00:08:25.080
According to Butch, the murders occurred&nbsp;
after he left for work. But forensics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:31.560
findings put the time of death between 2&nbsp;
and 4am. Since Butch claimed to have left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.560 --> 00:08:36.720
the house sometime after 4, he would&nbsp;
have been home during the murders.

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:45.660
Cornered by his own testimony, Butch immediately&nbsp;
changed it, claiming he was indeed at home when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.660 --> 00:08:50.580
Fallini murdered his family, but for some&nbsp;
reason the hitman had decided to spare him.

00:08:50.580 --> 00:08:55.920
It was an obvious lie - why had Butch&nbsp;
spent half the day feigning concern for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.920 --> 00:09:01.920
his family if he knew they were dead? Why&nbsp;
hadn't he reported the crime as soon as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:07.080
Fallini left? And for that matter,&nbsp;
why even lie in the first place?

00:09:07.080 --> 00:09:11.400
Unsurprisingly Butch had no&nbsp;
answer to any of these questions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.400 --> 00:09:17.820
and with his flimsy fable torn to shreds,&nbsp;
he made a full confession. In his own,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.820 --> 00:09:23.280
chilling words: "Once I started, I just&nbsp;
couldn't stop. It all went so fast."

00:09:23.280 --> 00:09:30.600
Detailing his deeds that morning, the sheer&nbsp;
evilness of Butch’s crime emerged. After murdering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.600 --> 00:09:36.600
his entire family in cold blood, Butch had had&nbsp;
a nice relaxing soak in the bath, got changed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.600 --> 00:09:41.700
then drove out of town to dispose of the murder&nbsp;
weapon and his blood stained clothes in a storm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.700 --> 00:09:47.100
drain. As we've already seen, in an attempt to&nbsp;
further cover his tracks he proceeded to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.100 --> 00:09:51.960
a completely normal day, going to work, hanging&nbsp;
out with his girlfriend and having a few drinks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.960 --> 00:09:57.840
with his mates, all while the bodies of his&nbsp;
entire family grew cold in their beds at home.

00:09:57.840 --> 00:10:06.420
Butch was put on trial for 6 counts of murder. His&nbsp;
defence lawyer, William Weber, tried to convince&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.420 --> 00:10:12.120
the jury Butch was innocent on the grounds&nbsp;
of insanity, but the ploy failed. Butch was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.120 --> 00:10:18.840
sentenced to 6 consecutive jail terms of 25 years,&nbsp;
one for each of the family members he’d slain.

00:10:19.980 --> 00:10:24.660
In many ways it was an open and shut case.&nbsp;
Along with Butch's confession there was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.660 --> 00:10:29.400
a whole boatload of evidence that he was&nbsp;
the killer. But when you think about it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.400 --> 00:10:32.040
several aspects of the crime don’t add up.

00:10:32.040 --> 00:10:38.400
Butch DeFeo fired 8 shots that night (a pair&nbsp;
for each parent, and one for each sibling),&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:43.080
and forensics confirmed the bodies&nbsp;
hadn't been moved post-mortem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.080 --> 00:10:47.940
meaning all 6 members of the&nbsp;
family were slain in their beds.

00:10:49.500 --> 00:10:56.700
As you probably noticed, guns are loud.&nbsp;
The house at 112 Ocean Avenue is pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.700 --> 00:11:00.840
big and the bedrooms are split across&nbsp;
two floors - it would have taken Butch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.840 --> 00:11:06.900
several minutes to move from room to room as he&nbsp;
systematically snuffed his nearest and dearest.

00:11:06.900 --> 00:11:12.420
Which leads us to a very obvious question&nbsp;
- how the hell did nobody wake up?

00:11:13.080 --> 00:11:17.280
Butch himself explained this little&nbsp;
conundrum by claiming to have laced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.280 --> 00:11:21.480
the family dinner with sleep-inducing&nbsp;
barbiturates, but no trace of any drugs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.480 --> 00:11:26.160
showed up in the toxicology reports. And&nbsp;
let’s not forget about the neighbours.

00:11:26.160 --> 00:11:30.540
In many films about the Amityville Horror,&nbsp;
the house is shown as being relatively remote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.540 --> 00:11:34.860
presumably to amp up the fear factor. In reality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.860 --> 00:11:38.220
it's on a regular street lined&nbsp;
with lots of other houses.

00:11:39.360 --> 00:11:43.620
During Butch's trial a reenactment of&nbsp;
the murders revealed that a single shot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.620 --> 00:11:46.800
from the murder weapon could&nbsp;
be heard a full 5 blocks away.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.580 --> 00:11:53.400
And yet, not a single neighbour heard&nbsp;
any of the *8* gunshots fired that night.

00:11:54.660 --> 00:12:00.720
There's also the question of motive - it was well&nbsp;
known in Amityville that Butch was bad news - he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.720 --> 00:12:07.560
drank heavily, took drugs, and committed several&nbsp;
petty crimes. He also had a truly terrible - and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.560 --> 00:12:12.180
sometimes violent - relationship with his&nbsp;
father, which had been deteriorating in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.180 --> 00:12:17.700
weeks leading up to the murders. But there was&nbsp;
no discernible motive for the other 5 killings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.700 --> 00:12:23.460
Butch was even said to be particularly close to&nbsp;
Dawn, the eldest of his four younger siblings.

00:12:23.460 --> 00:12:29.520
Even for someone like myself with a permanent&nbsp;
lease on the biggest tent in the sceptics camp,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.520 --> 00:12:37.320
it's all a bit strange. Add in the Lutz's&nbsp;
story of pervasive paranormal activity and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.320 --> 00:12:42.540
demons lurking in the dark, and you have&nbsp;
to ask yourself - is it possible that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.540 --> 00:12:48.960
the whole thing might actually be true? That&nbsp;
there's something... unnatural in that house?

00:12:48.960 --> 00:12:52.620
There's certainly some convincing evidence here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.360 --> 00:12:59.820
In 1979, George and Kathy Lutz volunteered&nbsp;
to a polygraph test to prove once and for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.820 --> 00:13:04.680
all that their story was true. They did things&nbsp;
properly too, recruiting Chris Gugas to carry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.680 --> 00:13:09.240
out the test (he was one of the leading&nbsp;
polygraph experts in America at the time).

00:13:09.240 --> 00:13:13.440
The couple were separately asked 5 questions&nbsp;
about their paranormal experiences at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.440 --> 00:13:19.500
house - ranging from a simple 'are you telling&nbsp;
the truth?' to the slightly more specific 'did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.500 --> 00:13:26.540
you levitate that one time?' , Gugas ultimately&nbsp;
concluded that both were telling the truth.

00:13:26.540 --> 00:13:30.360
If, on the other hand, you&nbsp;
happen to be a fellow sceptic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.360 --> 00:13:34.800
there’s ample reason to dismiss the&nbsp;
Amityville Horror as just another hoax.

00:13:34.800 --> 00:13:41.040
To start, several key scenes in the original book&nbsp;
are demonstrably inaccurate. The Catholic priest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.040 --> 00:13:46.320
who apparently got beaten up by a ghost while&nbsp;
trying to bless the house later swore in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.320 --> 00:13:51.660
court that he'd never even been there. And those&nbsp;
cloven-hoofed footprints that appeared in the snow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.660 --> 00:13:57.000
all around the house? Records show it didn't snow&nbsp;
anywhere near Amityville on the day in question.

00:13:58.140 --> 00:14:04.200
Then there's the infamous hidden 'red room,'&nbsp;
portrayed in the film as a gateway to hell.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.200 --> 00:14:10.320
Turns out it was merely a mundane storage&nbsp;
room with red paint on one of the walls,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.320 --> 00:14:15.840
and it was never 'hidden' as Kathy and George&nbsp;
Lutz claimed. The kids used to play there often,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.840 --> 00:14:22.140
which presumably wouldn't have been a great option&nbsp;
had it been a literal entrance to the underworld.

00:14:23.640 --> 00:14:31.080
Most damning of all - in 1979, William Weber&nbsp;
(Butch DeFeo’s defence attorney) confessed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.080 --> 00:14:38.100
concocting the whole thing with George and Kathy&nbsp;
Lutz over copious amounts of wine. Weber revealed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.100 --> 00:14:44.100
that it wasn't malevolent forces, but mounting&nbsp;
mortgage debts that convinced the Lutzs to flee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.100 --> 00:14:49.380
the house. They conjured all the ghosts and&nbsp;
goblins stuff to try and make some fast cash.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.880 --> 00:14:53.520
Considering they earned hundreds&nbsp;
of thousands of dollars from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.520 --> 00:14:56.460
Amityville horror, it’s safe&nbsp;
to say that went quite well.

00:14:57.000 --> 00:15:00.780
It's also worth pointing out that several other&nbsp;
people have lived in the house since the Lutzs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.780 --> 00:15:04.080
and not one of them has ever&nbsp;
reported anything unusual.

00:15:04.080 --> 00:15:09.000
In the decades since the Amityville Horror was&nbsp;
released, the story has become a money-making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.000 --> 00:15:15.900
behemoth, birthing over 25 films, dozens of&nbsp;
books, and a load of documentaries that I strongly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.900 --> 00:15:21.720
urge you not to bother watching. All these&nbsp;
retellings have created an awful lot of noise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.720 --> 00:15:28.500
and now there's so much fiction entwined with the&nbsp;
facts, it's difficult to know what to believe.

00:15:28.500 --> 00:15:32.280
Ultimately, there’s not a shred&nbsp;
of actual evidence that anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.280 --> 00:15:39.240
supernatural inhabits 112 Ocean Avenue&nbsp;
- now 108 Ocean Avenue - one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.240 --> 00:15:44.220
former owners changed the address in a&nbsp;
futile attempt to deter morbid tourists.

00:15:45.840 --> 00:15:50.340
It’s ironic that, amidst the tall&nbsp;
tales, most folk have forgotten the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.340 --> 00:15:56.100
genuine horror that haunts this house:&nbsp;
the brutal 1975 massacre of six people.

00:15:56.100 --> 00:16:05.640
Butch DeFeo died behind bars in March 2021, and&nbsp;
during his 46 years in prison, he implicated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.640 --> 00:16:12.420
everyone from, his sister Dawn, their mother&nbsp;
and a shadowy figure who mysteriously appeared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.420 --> 00:16:19.140
at the house and handed him a gun. However,&nbsp;
the exact details changed with each retelling.

00:16:19.140 --> 00:16:22.620
Ultimately, we may never know&nbsp;
the truth. But one thing is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:22.620 --> 00:16:28.260
for sure - what happened that night&nbsp;
in Amityville was truly horrifying.

00:16:28.260 --> 00:16:30.960
Thanks for watching.

